	    Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

        }
    }
}


A: Your assert statement seems fine. 
This Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX); will not pass if the last index of x in the old list is the same in the new list, i.e., when there are no more insertions of x in the new list. So it ensures that the last position of x in the old and the new lists are indeed different. 

But if you expect x to be "added" at a specific position every time you perform the PUT_InsertContract (assuming that the insert operation always adds the element at the end of the ArrayList), you can do the following assertion:
Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_index, New_arrListIndexOfX);

This assertion checks to see if the element was actually inserted at the specified index.
The Assert.AreEqual(Old_index, New_arrListIndexOfX); would pass if x was not inserted in the list, and it might be an assertion to ensure the add operation in ArrayList does not increase the size of the list when an operation is not permitted. 

So, there are some scenarios that might trigger the assertion false.
To ensure these scenarios you might want to add more tests that perform the insert in a position different from the count or just after a multiple inserts and ensure that it is actually performing an insert and not just permitting the addition. It would be better to analyze what is the expected behaviour of ArrayList for these cases.

A: When writing contract testing for ArrayList or any class, it's important to understand the contract of the method you're testing - what is expected after the method call.
Asserts should always test the expected outcome of the method call.


*

*In the Add method, the Add method should add an object to the end of the ArrayList and return an index of the new item.

*In the Remove method, the Remove method should remove the first occurrence of an object from the ArrayList and return a bool indicating whether object was found for removal.

*In the Insert method, the Insert method should insert an object into the ArrayList at a specific index.


Using these as your assumptions will help narrow down your contracts, while ensuring you're testing according to method contracts. 
As for your specific point about assertions, the assertion you have should indeed enforce your stated expectations:
Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX); 

This assertion passes if the indices are the same (i.e., the last occurrence of the value in the old and new lists is the same), which is not expected behavior for the Insert method. The assertion should pass if the method was able to insert the element at the new index.
Overall, your contracts and observations and assertions seem correct for these particular methods. Ensure you are testing with edge cases as well and you should be good. 
Good Luck.

A: As others have written, the assert should check whether the insert operation actually occurred by comparing the Old and New index locations of the inserted value. 
However, you should be aware of a corner-case. For ArrayList, .NET doesn't resize the array when an item is added at a certain index, it just shifts existing items to make room for the new item. Consequently, if you "remove" a value from the middle of the ArrayList by calling Remove or Insert, the count does not decrease. So LastIndexOf will still return the original location of the value just after removal/insertion, hence you might have a situation where New_arrListLastIndexOfX equals Old_arrListLastIndexOfX.
The fact that LastIndexOf is equal doesn't necessarily imply that value is no longer in the ArrayList: it can be in any position between index of the first occurrence at Old_arrListLastIndexOfX and index of the first occurrence at New_arrListLastIndexOfX. It could also mean the value is not present in New list at all.
This might be a situation where your assumptions are incorrectated. You might need to add more checks for example on the counts or contents of the lists to make sure the method works as expected.
You might want to investigate the behaviour of the Remove method and if you're able to control the state of the ArrayList before the Remove method call. It would help in verifying if the index of the element is correctly decreased when the item is removed from the ArrayList.
A better contract test for the Insert operation would include checking if the count has increased after the operation, checks whether the--------------------	    Assert.AreNotEqual(@@@ Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX); 
        }
    }   // End of Class
}   // End of Namespace


Here is one way that the test can be invoked:

 PUT_AddContract(ArrayList arrList, int x);
 PUT_RemoveContract(ArrayList arrList, int x);
 PUT_InsertContract(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index);


A: This is a problem of generating large test cases.
NUnit or Visual Studio Test runner may have been running out of memory when generating large test cases for this ArrayList test.
You can change the 'PexConfiguration' in your test project to increase the amount of code coverage your framework tries to achieve, by increasing the 'MaxBranches' value. But remember, you also increase the chance of generating large complex tests.
Also, you should make sure that you have enough RAM and other system resources (such as database connections, active timers etc.).

In order to further troubleshoot, you may want to use 'PexProfiler' to examine the behavior and performance of your tests, and to generate more detailed bug reports.

If you still encounter the same issue after the above solution, you need to check your Pex Configuration and try to analyze the failure further.
Please remember that you need to have the Pex Runner and also the 'Microsoft.Pex.Framework' assembly references in your test project.

Also try to run the Pex method in debug mode. Sometimes Pex can't find the test due to the environment path problems. It's always good to check the 'Pex.cs' output window after running a Pex method, especially when you get error message to find errors.
If you're running Pex on a 64-bit box and the test is failing and you're getting memory related error, check if Pex is configured for 32 bit. If not, you will need to configure it for 32 bit.

Please, let me know if this doesn't resolve your issue.
I hope some of this may be useful to you.

A: It seems that the ArrayList is not fully tested. There are some cases that the Pex framework is unable to handle. This could be due to multiple reasons. 
A few things you can try could be the following:

1. Try increasing the MaxStack depth or MaxBranches.
2. Try to add more checks for your assumptions.
3. Try to use a different set of inputs.
4. Consider using other Pex methods to generate the input for your current test.

Remember that Pex generates test inputs based on the method that it runs and the state of the object graph that the method operates upon, so even if it can't generate a specific input, it might still be able to generate other inputs that could cause that specific error.
You could check if the .pdb files for System.Core.dll, mscorlib.dll are there under test project and under the bin folder. If they are not, rebuild the solution (not just build), by ticking on the "rebuild solution" checkbox, you may need to attach the symbol(.pdb) file for those dll as they are required for debugging. Also ensure that you have the reference for Microsoft.Pex.Framework.
Moreover, make sure the framework is installed correctly and that the dll is properly referenced in your project.
Hope this helps.

A: Pex testing has its limitations in certain circumstances, especially when dealing with a specific class or built-in class like ArrayList, which can be unsupported or doesn't work correctly with Pex.
I suggest using Moles to replace the behavior of ArrayList. It will also provide you more control and help you isolate the code you want to test.
Moles is a dynamic analysis tool offered by Microsoft Pex/Stock and is used to write tests using Microsoft Visual Studio, Microsoft System Center alpha, or Enterprise Test, and Microsoft Pex/Stock. It can help you replace methods in your code and return specific values.
Here is an example:
using Moles.Framework;
using YourNamespace;

...

public void PUT_AddContract()
{
   var arrList = new YourArrayList();
   MArray listMoled = Moles.Double<ArrayList>.StaticInstance;
   listMoled.ToString = (a) => "Your expected result";
   ...
}

Mole allows you to change the behavior of a method for unit testing. To use Moles, you need to reference Microsoft.Moles.Framework and  Moles.--------------------        }

    }
